$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>Two decades from FIR to finality on dues</h2>
<p>The respondents, DDA employees, were among officials booked in 2001 over an alleged fraud in a slum survey. Sixteen years later the trial ended: four co-accused convicted on 28 February 2017, the respondents acquitted. They retired; the DDA withheld their gratuity under Rule 69(1)(c) of the CCS (Pension) Rules, pointing to the State&rsquo;s pending petition for leave to appeal against the acquittal under Section 378 CrPC.</p>
<p>The Central Administrative Tribunal directed release of the withheld dues against an undertaking: if the acquittal were ever reversed, the amounts would be refunded. The Delhi High Court dismissed the DDA&rsquo;s writ petitions on 25 March 2026. The DDA carried the matter to the Supreme Court by special leave — and lost there too, before a Bench of Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Prashant Kumar Mishra and Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Shree Chandrashekhar.</p>

<h2>The core propositions</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Acquittal is operative law.</strong> Until an appellate court disturbs it, &ldquo;the acquittal continues to hold the field.&rdquo; Pendency of a challenge changes nothing by itself.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>No stay, no suspension of rights.</strong> The State&rsquo;s leave petition carried no stay of the trial court&rsquo;s judgment; the employee&rsquo;s civil consequences therefore flow from the acquittal, not the appeal.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Undertakings protect the exchequer.</strong> The refund undertaking squares the circle: dues are released now, and public funds remain recoverable if the acquittal falls later.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Pensionary benefits cannot be illusory.</strong> Requiring retirees to await appellate timelines that can run a decade would hollow out the benefit itself.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court also dealt with precedent hygiene: it rejected the DDA&rsquo;s contention that the view taken in the Kishan Lal Taneja line was per incuriam, and distinguished the authority the DDA relied upon.</p>

<h2>Why this matters beyond service law</h2>
<p>The judgment is, at bottom, about the status of judicial orders pending appeal — a question that recurs across practice areas. Decrees, acquittals and awards are not provisional merely because someone has appealed; they are operative until stayed or reversed. Authorities who treat the filing of an appeal as a self-executing stay invert the appellate scheme, which deliberately places the burden of obtaining interim suspension on the challenger. For litigants on the winning side of any judgment, the case is a reminder to insist on that architecture: ask the withholding authority to identify the stay, and when none exists, the pendency argument should fail.</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Retired employees facing withheld dues should seek release against a refund undertaking — a formula this judgment now stamps with approval at the highest level.</p>
  <p>Employers and authorities should record, before withholding, whether any stay operates; mere pendency of a leave petition is not a defensible basis.</p>
  <p>Where the State&rsquo;s appeal eventually succeeds, the undertaking supplies the recovery route — parties should preserve it carefully in the record.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information. Entitlements under the pension rules depend on the specific rule, the employee&rsquo;s record and the stage of any connected proceedings.</div>
